<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indirect rule</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indirect rule is a method of government in which an imperial power governs through existing local rulers and institutions rather than replacing them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> ruled a wide periphery of tributary kings in this way, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Italian empires relied on local elites. British colonial rule preserved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> as instruments of indirect rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nineteenth-century colonial government combined a Dutch administrative superstructure with preserved indigenous aristocracies, notably the Javanese regents (bupati), a system of <b>indirect rule</b> that maintained order efficiently but shielded the colonial state from any genuine accountability to the governed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Local rule followed Frederick Lugard's <b>indirect rule</b>, which worked through chiefs; they had ruled by consent and could be destooled by their elders, but under the colonial government they looked to Accra for decisions, and many came to see themselves as an aristocracy rewarded with honours and knighthoods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gupta administrative system was deliberately decentralised, combining direct imperial administration of the core Gangetic territories with <b>indirect rule</b> over a wide periphery of feudatory kings who retained internal autonomy in exchange for tribute and military service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>British <b>indirect rule</b> after 1903 deliberately preserved the emirate system's administrative structure, recognising its institutional sophistication, with consequences for the political structure of northern Nigeria that remain visible today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…governors bearing titles such as fari or farma, often royal kinsmen or trusted commanders rather than local hereditary chiefs, the most senior being the kurmina-fari who governed the western provinces; this marked a departure from Mali's more <b>indirect rule</b> and gave Songhai greater cohesion over its territorial core.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>indirect rule</b>, set out by Frederick Lugard in The Dual Mandate in British Tropical Africa (1922), existing rulers such as the emirs of the Sokoto Caliphate in Northern Nigeria kept their courts and taxes under British supervision, as the Indian princely states did under the Raj; where…</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
